> How the continuous variables are treated under wlsmv?

I thought I answered that above.  They are treated as continuous.  It might help to be more specific.  WLSMV is not a thing, it is shorthand for 3 things:

estimator = "DWLS"
se = "robust.sem"
test = "scaled.shifted" # "mean.var.adjusted" also available, which is what "MV" stands for

You can request estimator = "wls" for models with only continuous variables, but that is the ADF estimator described by Browne (1984) <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.2044-8317.1984.tb00789.x>, not the 3-stage estimator for models with any ordered variables for which we must assume a latent underlying distribution:

estimate thresholds from 1-way contingency tables
estimate polychoric/serial correlations from thresholds and 2-way tables
fit SEM to polychoric/serial matrix
The robust "MV" correction is to account for the uncertainty of estimates in steps 1 and 2 when fitting the model in step 3.  That is not an issue for (full) WLS/ADF with continuous variables only.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

